# Files in the repo that are not the website.
#
# The asset directory is the repo root, so without this list every one of these
# is uploaded and publicly served. That is what was happening: build-site.py,
# BRAND.md and the client's print artwork were all fetchable over HTTPS.
#
# Syntax is gitignore-style, matched against paths inside the asset directory.
# Anything a page links to must NOT appear here; tools/validate.py enforces that
# by resolving every referenced file against these patterns.
#
# Read wrangler's own behaviour before trusting a default: it walks the asset
# directory with a plain recursive readdir and excludes only `.assetsignore`,
# `_headers` and `_redirects` on its own. Dotfiles are NOT excluded. The
# "Read N files from the assets directory" line it prints is the raw walk from
# BEFORE this file is applied, and it counts directories too, so it is not a
# measure of what gets uploaded and should not be read as one.

# The git directory. Not a default exclusion, and the asset directory is the
# repo root, so without this line every object in the history is uploaded and
# served: /.git/config, every past commit, anything ever committed and removed.
.git/

# Local wrangler state and installed packages, if either ever appears here.
.wrangler/
node_modules/

# Generators, the validator and the test suite.
tools/

# Worker source. It is the entry point, not an asset, and serving a copy of it
# next to the running code is pointless at best.
worker/
wrangler.jsonc

# Documentation and handover notes.
*.md

# Master artwork and print deliverables: the logo library and the QR codes.
# These are for the client and the printer, not for the web. robots.txt already
# asked crawlers to leave assets/brand alone; this stops it being served at all.
assets/brand/
brand-assets/

# Local development scaffolding.
.dev.vars.example
.gitignore
